WEBVTT

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:31.960
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Chana Joffe-Walt sitting in for Ira Glass. Ira is here, but I'm taking over today's show because we're talking about questions that I think are pretty fundamental to all of us at various points in our lives but are especially pressing for me these days. I'd like to explain with a brief example of a situation I find myself in all the time lately, with my two children, feeling deeply lost.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《美国生活》。我是Chana Joffe-Walt，代替Ira Glass主持。Ira其实就在现场，但今天由我来接管节目，因为我们正在讨论一些我认为在人生各个阶段对我们每个人都至关重要，但最近对我而言尤为紧迫的问题。我想通过一个我最近常遇到的、让我感到极其迷茫的、关于我两个孩子的例子来解释这一点。

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:41.260
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's bath time. Jacob, the older one, is running a restaurant. He's giving out great prizes-- cups for grownups, and also animals.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>现在是洗澡时间。大一点的儿子Jacob在开一家餐馆。他正在发放很棒的奖品——大人的杯子，还有动物玩偶。

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.000
<v Jacob>Animals.
<v Jacob>动物。

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.730
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I want to hug an animal.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我想抱抱那个动物。

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:49.320
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Jacob says, if someone in your family is Christian, you can get one of those.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Jacob说，如果你家里有人是基督徒，你就可以拿一个。

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:55.150
<v Jacob>But if somebody in your family is Christian, you're going to give those to them.
<v Jacob>但是如果有人是基督徒，你就要把那些给他们。

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:55.640
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>什么？

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.550
<v Jacob>Is your family Christian?
<v Jacob>你们家是基督徒吗？

00:00:58.550 --> 00:00:59.040
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>No.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>不是。

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.480
<v Jacob>Is your mom or your dad Christian?
<v Jacob>你妈妈或爸爸是基督徒吗？

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.180
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>No.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>不是。

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.480
<v Jacob>Is your brother or your sister Christian?
<v Jacob>你哥哥或姐姐是基督徒吗？

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.360
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>No. They're all Jewish.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>不是。他们都是犹太人。

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.980
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>He snatches the animal and says, sorry.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他一把抢过动物，说，抱歉。

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:16.650
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Wait. I can't have any animals unless I'm Christian?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>等等。除非我是基督徒，否则我就不能拿任何动物吗？

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.740
<v Jacob>You can't have that duck, sorry.
<v Jacob>你不能拿那只鸭子，抱歉。

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:19.940
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Why can't I have the duck?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>为什么我不能拿那只鸭子？

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:23.430
<v Jacob>Because you're not Christian.
<v Jacob>因为你不是基督徒。

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:42.590
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>No Jews allowed, says my 3 and 1/2-year-old to my Jewish face. So here is my question-- what am I supposed to say right here? Because I should say something, right? I should say, that is not OK, because-- why?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>“不准犹太人入内”，我3岁半的儿子对着我这张犹太人的脸说道。那么问题来了——我这时候该说什么？因为我得说点什么，对吧？我应该说，那是不对的，因为——为什么呢？

00:01:42.590 --> 00:02:03.350
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Because I said so? Because you're a Jew? Because Jews like your great grandparents were driven from their communities by exactly the mentality you've just expressed to me, my 3 and 1/2-year-old boy? Or because we live in a majority Christian country, and I'm worried that I've clearly already failed to provide you with a positive relationship to Judaism?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>因为我说了算吗？因为你是犹太人吗？因为犹太人（比如你的曾祖父母）正是被你刚才对我——你3岁半的儿子——所表达的那种心态赶出了社区？还是因为我们生活在一个以基督教为主的国家，我担心我显然已经没能让你与犹太教建立起积极的关系？

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:11.920
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Or is the best approach not to say anything? He's just having a bath. I don't know, as you will hear.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>还是说最好的做法就是什么都不说？他只是在洗澡。我不知道，你会听到的。

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.990
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But sweetie, it's not nice to say that somebody can't have something because they're not Christian.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但是宝贝，说别人因为不是基督徒就不能拿东西，这样是不礼貌的。

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:27.970
<v Jacob>Well, you can't have the animals because you're not Christian.
<v Jacob>嗯，你不能拿这些动物，因为你不是基督徒。

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.000
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That makes me feel sad, though, sweetie.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但那让我感到难过，宝贝。

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.920
<v Jacob>Well, many people who are Christian can have those animals. In this restaurant, that's what the rules are.
<v Jacob>嗯，许多基督徒可以拥有那些动物。在这个餐馆里，这就是规则。

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That's not kind, though.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但那不友善。

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:44.250
<v Jacob>Yeah, but that's what the rules are at this restaurant.
<v Jacob>是的，但这就是这家餐馆的规则。

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:48.230
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Well, there's so many rules in this anti-Semitic restaurant.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>好吧，这家反犹太餐馆里的规矩可真多。

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:53.230
<v Jacob>Sorry. They're kind for some people.
<v Jacob>抱歉。它们对某些人是友善的。

00:02:53.230 --> 00:03:12.450
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>He says, they're kind for some people, just not my kind of people. The problem with talking to kids about anything that's actually important is that they don't know anything. It's your job to teach them about all of the stuff that matters most, but you have nothing to work with.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他说，它们对某些人是友善的，只是不对我这类人友善。和孩子们谈论真正重要的事情时，问题在于他们什么都不懂。你的工作是教他们所有最重要的事情，但你却没有什么素材可用。

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:17.910
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>They don't know religion. They don't know history. They don't know how to maintain proper hygiene.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他们不懂宗教。他们不懂历史。他们不知道如何保持正确的个人卫生。

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:32.650
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Also a problem, they're little, and they have feelings. So you have to be careful about saying, well, let me tell you a story about a man named Adolf Hitler. He would have liked the way you run your restaurant, by the way.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>还有一个问题，他们年纪很小，而且有情感。所以你得小心，不能说，好吧，让我给你讲一个关于阿道夫·希特勒的男人的故事。顺便说一句，他会很喜欢你经营餐馆的方式。

00:03:32.650 --> 00:04:18.330
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>We've all been on the kid side of this kind of conversation, where a grownup is fumbling through something that is clearly important. The big one most of us remember is when someone told us about sex. And on the adult side, whatever the question is-- what is sex, what is God, or what is ISIS, what does transgendered mean-- you know in that moment that what you say could really matter, could be the thing this person remembers and carries with them into adulthood, could shape the way they feel about their safety or their sexuality. These conversations are how we make our mark on the next generation. They're also, very often, how we learn how much we do not know.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们都曾作为孩子经历过这种对话，当时一个成年人在笨拙地处理某件明显很重要的事情。我们大多数人记得最清楚的一件大事是有人告诉我们关于性的事。而在成年人这边，无论问题是什么——什么是性，什么是上帝，或者什么是ISIS，变性意味着什么——在那一刻你知道你所说的话真的很重要，可能会成为这个人记住并带入成年生活的东西，可能会影响他们对自身安全或性的感受。这些对话是我们给下一代打上烙印的方式。它们也经常是我们学习到自己有多么无知的方式。

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:53.450
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And in today's show, we're going to listen in as people take on three of the most difficult things to talk about. We're going to hear teenagers talking very frankly to each other about sex, parents talking to their children about race, and kids talking to each other about death. Today's show, "The Birds and the Bees." We've got Jonathan Goldstein, comedian W. Kamau Bell. Stay with us.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在今天的节目中，我们将聆听人们如何面对三个最难谈论的话题。我们将听到青少年非常坦率地谈论性，父母与孩子谈论种族，以及孩子们谈论死亡。今天的节目是《鸟儿与蜜蜂》。嘉宾有Jonathan Goldstein和喜剧演员W. Kamau Bell。请继续收听。

00:04:53.450 --> 00:05:19.860
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Act One, "Some Like It Not, On the Neck." To begin, I should say, throughout this hour we're going to be talking about subject matter that can be difficult to discuss with children. So if you've got kids listening, that's something to consider. Also in this next story, as Ira says, we will be acknowledging the existence of sex and talking a lot about our reluctance to speak of its existence.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>第一幕，《有些人不喜欢，在脖子上》。首先，我应该说明，在接下来的一小时里，我们将讨论一些难以与孩子沟通的主题。所以如果你有孩子在听，这是需要考虑的。同样在下一个故事里，正如Ira所说，我们将承认性的存在，并谈论我们对谈论其存在的抵触。

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:41.700
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>OK, let's start with the beginning. One way a lot of kids learn about the birds and bees is from each other. A couple weeks ago, a seven-year-old named Moxie told me a friend of hers, this kid, Aaron, said something to her that bothered her on the bus. He said, if he drew a picture of a baby on a piece of paper and put it in her belly, that would make a baby.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>好的，我们从头开始。很多孩子了解“鸟儿与蜜蜂”的方式是通过彼此。几周前，一个叫Moxie的七岁女孩告诉我，她的朋友Aaron在校车上对她说了一些困扰她的话。他说，如果他在纸上画一个婴儿，然后把它放进她的肚子里，那就会生出一个婴儿。

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:49.480
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Moxie knew this was incorrect. That's why it bothered her. So a week later at the playground across the street from her house, she sat Aaron down.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Moxie知道这是不对的。这就是为什么她感到困扰。所以一周后，在她家对面的操场上，她让Aaron坐下来。

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:54.550
<v Moxie>The situation about the paper not working thing.
<v Moxie>关于那张纸不管用的情况。

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:56.420
<v Aaron>What's that means?
<v Aaron>那是什么意思？

00:05:56.420 --> 00:06:01.820
<v Moxie>On the bus a few days ago, you talked about paper and body and about a baby.
<v Moxie>几天前在校车上，你谈到了纸和身体，还有关于婴儿的事。

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:03.160
<v Aaron>Oh, right, right, right.
<v Aaron>哦，对，对，对。

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:06.750
<v Moxie>That was a silly plan.
<v Moxie>那是个愚蠢的计划。

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.260
<v Aaron>I'm sure it's going to work, but I don't know.
<v Aaron>我确定它会成功的，但我不知道。

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:19.700
<v Moxie>Uh, I'm not sure, because the baby can't survive, you see? I'm just saying.
<v Moxie>呃，我不确定，因为婴儿无法存活，你明白吗？我只是随口一说。

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:20.280
<v Aaron>Sorry.
<v Aaron>抱歉。

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:30.810
<v Moxie>You need to make a sperm. And the sperm somehow has to get into my body. And that's how it works.
<v Moxie>你需要制造精子。精子必须以某种方式进入我的身体。这就是它的运作方式。

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:32.650
<v Aaron>I did not know that.
<v Aaron>我不知道这一点。

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:34.930
<v Moxie>Well, now you do.
<v Moxie>好吧，现在你知道了。

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.450
<v Aaron>Sorry.
<v Aaron>抱歉。

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:58.190
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When it comes to sex, a lot of us learn the wrong thing first and the right thing later. You learn through a series of corrections. You start to find out that half the things your brother told you were not right, that that scene in the movie is not realistic, that [? Sophia De Santos ?] was lying when she said she did that thing, because that thing is not possible.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当谈到性时，我们很多人先学到了错误的东西，后来才学到正确的。你是通过一系列的纠正来学习的。你开始发现你哥哥告诉你的事情有一半是不对的，电影里的那一幕并不现实，[Sophia De Santos]说她做了那件事是在撒谎，因为那件事根本不可能发生。

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:15.930
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That's basically our system. You learn things wrong, test them out, and hopefully get the real story from someone named Moxie. This begins on the playground and just continues all the way until you actually start having sex, and all the weird information that was not corrected begins to matter.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这基本上就是我们的系统。你学到了错误的东西，去尝试它们，希望从一个叫Moxie的人那里得到真实的故事。这始于操场，一直持续到你真正开始发生性关系，而所有那些未经纠正的怪异信息开始变得重要为止。

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.390
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What are you hoping they're going to cover?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你希望他们涵盖什么内容？

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:19.980
<v Deondre>I hope they cover everything.
<v Deondre>我希望他们涵盖所有内容。

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:28.650
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Deondre is in a classroom at Buffalo State with about 50 other people, mostly men, mostly his fraternity brothers. He's here for some sex ed.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Deondre在布法罗州立大学的一间教室里，周围大约有50人，大多数是男性，大多数是他的兄弟会成员。他来这里是为了接受一些性教育。

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:30.750
<v Deondre>I feel like some stuff people don't know.
<v Deondre>我觉得有些东西人们不知道。

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.680
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What don't you know?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你不知道什么？

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:35.120
<v Deondre>I don't know. That's a good question, you know.
<v Deondre>我不知道。这是个好问题，你知道的。

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:47.960
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is a voluntary workshop about sexual assault and consent, on a Monday night. You'd think this event might not win out over, say, hitting up the Jamba Juice or staring at your van Gogh poster.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这是一个关于性侵犯和同意的自愿研讨会，在周一晚上。你可能会觉得这个活动比去喝果汁或者盯着你的梵高海报更有吸引力。

00:07:47.960 --> 00:08:00.090
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But this room is packed. The students are wide awake. Knees are bouncing. Hands are shooting up in the air. The instructor, Paula Madrigal, is talking about the importance of consent.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但这个房间挤满了人。学生们都很清醒。膝盖在抖动。手纷纷举起。讲师Paula Madrigal正在谈论同意的重要性。

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:09.890
<v Paula Madrigal>So consent is a verbal and enthusiastic yes, OK? Why is it enthusiastic?
<v Paula Madrigal>所以，同意是一个口头的、热情的“是”，好吗？为什么要是热情的？

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:11.440
<v Student 1>Because you actually want to do it.
<v Student 1>因为你真的想做。

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:15.810
<v Paula Madrigal>Exactly. You want to do it. And sex should be fun, right?
<v Paula Madrigal>完全正确。你真的想做。而且性应该是快乐的，对吧？

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.150
<v Student 2>Yeah.
<v Student 2>是的。

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:20.290
<v Paula Madrigal>Yeah. I hope so.
<v Paula Madrigal>是的。我希望如此。

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:42.690
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This kind of workshop was repeated in classrooms all across campus here and throughout the country in the last year. College administrators are panicked about sexual assault, as they should be. So they're turning to this kind of education as the answer instead of mostly focusing on protecting women, things like rape whistles or travel with a buddy programs.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>过去一年里，这种研讨会在全国各地的校园教室里反复进行。大学管理者对性侵犯感到恐慌，他们理应如此。所以他们转向这种教育作为答案，而不是主要专注于保护女性，比如防狼哨或结伴同行计划。

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:53.250
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>They also want to teach men actual sentences to say. Do you like this? Can I kiss you? But the students here are struggling with the basic premise.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他们还想教男学生实际要说的话。你喜欢这样吗？我可以吻你吗？但这里的学生们正在努力理解这个基本前提。

00:08:53.250 --> 00:09:02.600
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>They have to learn to speak in this new way because if you don't you're a rapist? Because it's just more virtuous? They have questions right from the start.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他们必须学习用这种新方式说话，因为如果你不这样做，你就是强奸犯？因为这只是更高尚？他们从一开始就有疑问。

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:08.230
<v Paula Madrigal>It's a continuous process of checking up, or checking in, either/or. What does that mean?
<v Paula Madrigal>这是一个持续的检查过程，或者说确认过程，两者皆有。那是什么意思？

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:10.230
<v Student 2>You keep asking her? That's weird.
<v Student 2>你一直问她？那太怪了。

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:22.320
<v Student 3>That kind of messes up the mood. I'm not trying to be funny, but if she says yes, and you know, she keeps saying, yeah, I've got to be like, hey, you sure? Are you really sure? We're about to do this.
<v Student 3>那样会破坏气氛。我不是想搞笑，但如果她说好，而且你知道，她一直说，好，我得像这样，嘿，你确定吗？你真的确定吗？我们要开始做了。

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:23.150
<v Student 4>Every 10 minutes.
<v Student 4>每10分钟一次。

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:29.960
<v Student 3>I'm almost there. I'm about to go to the drawer to get the protection. No, no. You're right. You're right. You're right.
<v Student 3>我快到了。我正要走到抽屉去拿避孕套。不，不。你是对的。你是对的。你是对的。

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.270
<v Student 2>And you're like, no.
<v Student 2>然后你就像，不。

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:43.360
<v Student 3>No. Obviously, I'm going to stop. But the re-affirmation of yes over and over again, that kind of would mess up the mood. So I feel like should there be a number of times that you should-- you know?
<v Student 3>不。显然，我会停下来。但反复的“同意”再确认，那样会破坏气氛。所以我感觉，是不是应该有一个次数限制……你知道吗？

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:55.600
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>He presses them on this. Two? Three? Seven? But that guy is overtaken by his fraternity brothers, who also have urgent questions. Does body language count as consent?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他在此事上追问他们。两次？三次？七次？但那个家伙被他的兄弟会成员抢了话，他们也有紧急的问题。肢体语言算作同意吗？

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:07.360
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What if she said yes before you got into the room when you were both sober, like in a message on Twitter or something? How many beers is too much to have consent? Again, please offer a number.
