00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:58.730
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And if you aren't careful, you drown in the sorrow of the black experience in America. Damn. We live in Berkeley. People have been protesting the police blocks from our house. How do I explain that?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>而且如果你不小心，你会淹死在美国黑人经历的悲伤中。该死。我们住在伯克利。人们一直在我们家几个街区外抗议警察。我该怎么解释呢？

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:10.720
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This was on my mind recently, how to teach Sami about racism, when this thing happened. It was January 26 of this year, my birthday. I was having a low key celebration, just like I like them.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这是我最近在思考的问题，如何向Sami解释种族主义，当时发生了那件事。那是今年的1月26日，我的生日。我正在进行低调的庆祝，就像我喜欢的那样。

00:21:10.720 --> 00:26:15.230
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I was carrying a children's book I had just bought for Sami, The Case for Loving: The Fight for Interracial Marriage. Mildred and Richard Loving are the interracial couple responsible for the Supreme Court striking down the laws in 16 states that banned interracial marriage. And if I was going to get into racism with Sami, then the Loving story-- which was about a family like ours-- seemed like a great place to start.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我拿着一本我刚为Sami买的儿童书，《爱的理由：争取跨种族婚姻的斗争》。Mildred和Richard Loving是一对跨种族夫妇，他们促使最高法院推翻了16个州禁止跨种族婚姻的法律。如果我要和Sami谈论种族主义，那么Loving的故事——关于一个像我们这样的家庭——似乎是一个很好的开始。

00:26:15.230 --> 00:26:20.840
<v Moderator>So I'm sure that many of you have heard the story of the incident that happened on January 26.
<v Moderator>所以我相信你们很多人都听说过1月26日发生的那起事件的故事。

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:44.030
<v W. Kamau Bell>On a Friday in March, Melissa and I sat on a stage at a middle school in a packed auditorium. My main concern was that somehow all the planning and structure was going to lead to an event that was careful, sanitized, and maybe the worst thing, boring. It wasn't, due in large part to two amazing women on the panel, Kadijah Means-- who we'll get to in a minute-- but first, Nikki Jones.
<v W. Kamau Bell>在三月的一个周五，Melissa和我坐在中学礼堂的一个舞台上，台下坐满了人。我主要担心的是所有的计划和结构会导向一个小心翼翼、被消毒过，或许是最糟糕的——无聊的活动。但事实并非如此，这在很大程度上要归功于小组中的两位杰出女性，Kadijah Means——我们待会儿会谈到她——但首先是Nikki Jones。

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:47.020
<v Nikki Jones>OK. I'll put on my professor voice.
<v Nikki Jones>好的。我开启我的教授模式。

00:26:47.020 --> 00:27:04.730
<v W. Kamau Bell>Nikki Jones teaches at UC Berkeley. She said something that, for me, made sense of the world, not just about the small racial injustices that happen in cafes but the bigger, deadly ones that are happening all around America. She asked us to think of the world in terms of black space and white space.
<v W. Kamau Bell>Nikki Jones在加州大学伯克利分校任教。她说了一些对我来说理清了这个世界，不仅是关于咖啡馆里发生的小种族不公，还有美国各地正在发生的更宏大、更致命的不公。她让我们从黑人空间和白人空间的角度去思考这个世界。

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:19.910
<v W. Kamau Bell>She credits Elijah Anderson for this theory, but tonight she made it her main point. Nikki says people have ideas about black spaces. It's tied to blackness and poverty and the ghetto. It's impossible to say the word ghetto in America without thinking about black people.
<v W. Kamau Bell>她将这个理论归功于Elijah Anderson，但今晚她把它作为重点。Nikki说人们对黑人空间有某种看法。它与黑人身份、贫困和贫民窟挂钩。在美国，不说贫民窟这个词而不想起黑人是不可能的。

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:31.060
<v W. Kamau Bell>See a black man in a suit, your brain does a calculus. What poor neighborhood did he or his parents rise up out of, or his parents' parents? It all goes back to the ghetto.
<v W. Kamau Bell>看到一个穿西装的黑人，你的大脑会进行计算。他或他的父母、他父母的父母是从哪个贫穷街区走出来的？这一切都回到了贫民窟。

00:27:31.060 --> 00:27:47.200
<v Nikki Jones>And the ghetto itself is believed to be carried on the bodies, on and in black people. And that presents special dilemmas when black people are in white space. And what's white space? Well, just about everywhere else. Right?
<v Nikki Jones>而贫民窟本身被认为存在于身体上，存在于黑人的身上和内心。这在黑人处于白人空间时带来了特殊的困境。什么是白人空间？好吧，几乎所有其他地方。对吧？

00:27:47.200 --> 00:28:08.510
<v W. Kamau Bell>She says UC Berkeley, where she teaches, white space. Almost all education, public and private, white space. Movie theaters, restaurants, some of my own stand-up shows-- I'm looking at you, Spokane-- white space. Public Radio, white space. And of course, the Elmwood cafe.
<v W. Kamau Bell>她说加州大学伯克利分校，她任教的地方，是白人空间。几乎所有的教育，公立和私立，都是白人空间。电影院、餐馆、我自己的一些单口喜剧表演——我看着你，斯波坎——白人空间。公共广播，白人空间。当然，还有Elmwood咖啡馆。

00:28:08.510 --> 00:28:20.940
<v Nikki Jones>So in these white spaces, black people have a special burden, and they face a number of dilemmas. They have to prove that they belong there. The burden is on them to prove that they belong in a particular space.
<v Nikki Jones>所以在这些白人空间里，黑人背负着特殊的负担，他们面临着许多困境。他们必须证明自己属于那里。证明自己属于某个特定空间的重担在他们身上。

00:28:20.940 --> 00:28:29.860
<v Nikki Jones>Now let's go back to the incident. Who belongs in that space? What is the presumption about what Kamau is carrying on his body?
<v Nikki Jones>现在回到那个事件。谁属于那个空间？关于Kamau身上背负着什么有什么假设？

00:28:29.860 --> 00:28:52.180
<v W. Kamau Bell>Of course, not belonging can work both ways. A white person in a black space lacks a similar trustworthiness. But think of how rare it is that a white person goes to a black space. Like a tourist in another country, you can leave whenever you choose.
<v W. Kamau Bell>当然，不属于某种空间也可能双向运作。一个处于黑人空间的白人缺乏类似的可信度。但想想白人去黑人空间是多么罕见。就像在另一个国家的游客，你可以随时离开。

00:28:52.180 --> 00:29:05.740
<v W. Kamau Bell>But the highlight of the night, high school senior, Kadijah Means. She's 18 and made a name for herself in the Bay Area as an activist. She's an awesome public speaker who, importantly to me, knows how to open with a joke.
<v W. Kamau Bell>但今晚的亮点是高中四年级学生，Kadijah Means。她18岁，在湾区作为活动家而出名。她是一位出色的演讲者，重要的是对我来说，她知道如何以笑话开场。

00:29:05.740 --> 00:29:17.120
<v Kadijah Means>So number one, I want to say that I've been black all of my life. That's a pretty good amount of motivation for me to be a social justice advocate.
<v Kadijah Means>所以第一点，我想说我一辈子都是黑人。这是我成为社会正义倡导者的相当好的动力。

00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:23.520
<v W. Kamau Bell>And then she said something that made me take extra notice. She talked about being younger than my daughter, Sami, when she learned about race.
<v W. Kamau Bell>然后她说了些让我格外注意的话。她谈到了在比我女儿Sami还小的时候就了解到了种族。

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:37.310
<v Kadijah Means>I learned about race at a really young age, maybe like two or three. My dad has a really good way of teaching about fractions. When he would make me toast, he would cut it in half and be like, this is half. This is a fourth.
<v Kadijah Means>我在很小的时候就了解了种族，也许两三岁左右。我爸爸有一个教分数的很好方法。当他给我做吐司时，他会把它切成两半，说，这是二分之一。这是四分之一。

00:29:37.310 --> 00:29:49.330
<v Kadijah Means>So I learned about race in a kind of similar way, learning that white didn't mean white like paper, but it was a construct. Yeah, I learned that pretty young. That was weird.
<v Kadijah Means>所以我以类似的方式了解了种族，了解到白色并不意味着像纸那样的白色，而是一个建构。是的，我年纪很小就知道了。那很奇怪。

00:29:49.330 --> 00:29:58.830
<v W. Kamau Bell>My oldest daughter is four. She has just begun to understand that people are different colors. I can't imagine somehow connecting that to the fact that race itself is a construct.
<v W. Kamau Bell>我的大女儿四岁。她刚刚开始理解人们的肤色不同。我无法想象将此与种族本身是一种建构这一事实联系起来。

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:09.290
<v W. Kamau Bell>I'm not sure I could define the word construct to another adult, let alone a four-year-old. Kadijah then closed with a few tips on how we can all be less racist, like this one.
<v W. Kamau Bell>我不确定我能否向另一个成年人定义“建构”这个词，更不用说四岁小孩了。Kadijah随后总结了几条关于我们如何才能减少种族主义的建议，比如这条。

00:30:09.290 --> 00:30:16.790
<v Kadijah Means>Focus less on color blindness, because honestly, you're not going to get a gold star for that. Be more color competent. Be more--
<v Kadijah Means>少关注色盲，因为老实说，你不会因此得到金星。多一点颜色胜任力。多一点——

00:30:16.790 --> 00:30:18.040
<v Kadijah Means>[APPLAUSE]
<v Kadijah Means>[掌声]

00:30:18.040 --> 00:30:18.880
<v Kadijah Means>Thanks.
<v Kadijah Means>谢谢。

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:19.720
<v W. Kamau Bell>And this one.
<v W. Kamau Bell>还有这条。

00:30:19.720 --> 00:30:29.640
<v Kadijah Means>Please don't call people articulate. Don't come up to me after and tell me I'm articulate. Please do not do that. And there's a whole bunch of reasons why that we don't have time for.
<v Kadijah Means>请不要称人为“口齿伶俐”。不要在之后过来告诉我我很口齿伶俐。请不要那样做。这有很多原因，我们没有时间解释。

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:32.750
<v W. Kamau Bell>You might want to grab a pen and some paper, because she's on a roll.
<v W. Kamau Bell>你可能想拿支笔和纸，因为她正说得起劲。

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:40.740
<v Kadijah Means>Your mind makes generalizations. That's what it does. It's not bad to that. It's bad to think that that one generalization is what everyone is.
<v Kadijah Means>你的思想会做出概括。那就是它所做的。概括本身并不坏。坏的是认为那一个概括就是所有人。

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:52.670
<v Kadijah Means>You also have to take a step outside of that and remember that people are individuals. People have different lives. Just because I'm black doesn't mean I act like other black people. Black people built this country, whether you like it or not. Love us, please. Thank you.
<v Kadijah Means>你也必须从中走出来，记住人们是独立的个体。人们有不同的生活。仅仅因为我是黑人并不意味着我的行为像其他黑人。黑人建立了这个国家，不管你喜欢与否。请爱我们。谢谢。

00:30:52.670 --> 00:30:58.830
<v Kadijah Means>[APPLAUSE]
<v Kadijah Means>[掌声]

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:10.920
<v W. Kamau Bell>I was so impressed by Kadijah. I began to think about her dad, the one who taught her about fractions and race. Kadijah was completely versed in the evils of racism. But she was also idealistic about the future.
<v W. Kamau Bell>我对Kadijah印象深刻。我开始思考她的父亲，那个教她分数和种族的人。Kadijah对种族主义的罪恶非常精通。但她对未来也很理想化。

00:31:10.920 --> 00:31:24.990
<v W. Kamau Bell>She understood how well-intended people could do racist things, but how that doesn't always make them bad people. She got the broad strokes and the nuances. And Kadijah also knew how to do something that is absolutely essential to your survival as a black person in America.
<v W. Kamau Bell>她理解善意的人如何可能做出种族主义行为，但这并不总是意味着他们是坏人。她把握了大局和细微差别。而且Kadijah也知道如何做一件对你在美国的黑人身份的生存至关重要的事情。

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:44.930
<v W. Kamau Bell>She saw and identified racism in her everyday life, and she didn't internalize it. But she also didn't ignore it. She just judo flipped it and kept walking. I wanted some advice on how to raise a kid like that. Who was this dad? I imagined him to be a mix of '60s era Berkeley hippie and hope and change era Obama.
<v W. Kamau Bell>她在日常生活中看到并识别出种族主义，她没有内化它。但她也没有忽视它。她只是像柔道一样将其翻转，然后继续前行。我想得到一些关于如何抚养那样一个孩子的建议。这位父亲是谁？我想象他是一个60年代伯克利嬉皮士和“希望与变革”时代奥巴马的混合体。

00:31:44.930 --> 00:31:48.780
<v Cliff Means>I don't smile a lot. So I'm not the smiling black man.
<v Cliff Means>我不常笑。所以我不是那个爱笑的黑人。

00:31:48.780 --> 00:31:55.150
<v W. Kamau Bell>Yeah. That's Cliff Means. He's a self-described businessman who means business.
<v W. Kamau Bell>是的。那是Cliff Means。他是一个自称“讲生意”的商人。

00:31:55.150 --> 00:32:08.260
<v W. Kamau Bell>I like to imagine that his business card reads on one line, "Cliff Means," and under that, "Business." In fact, when we ask Cliff to do the interview, initially he said, "I don't know you. I don't know your audience. So no."
<v W. Kamau Bell>我喜欢想象他的名片上写着一行“Cliff Means”，下面写着“生意”。事实上，当我们请Cliff做采访时，起初他说，“我不认识你。我不了解你的听众。所以不行。”

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:16.890
<v W. Kamau Bell>So when my producer, Robyn, and I met with Cliff and Kadijah, I knew he was still reluctant, but I wasn't sure what had changed his mind. I had to ask.
<v W. Kamau Bell>所以当我的制作人Robyn和我与Cliff和Kadijah见面时，我知道他仍然很勉强，但我不知道是什么让他改变了主意。我不得不问。

00:32:16.890 --> 00:32:20.080
<v W. Kamau Bell>Why are you doing this interview, then?
<v W. Kamau Bell>那为什么你要接受这次采访？

00:32:20.080 --> 00:32:35.440
<v Cliff Means>Kadijah really wanted me to do this is the bottom line. Now, had I found out anything too horrific about either one of you, then it probably still would have been no. But I couldn't find anything terrible.
<v Cliff Means>Kadijah真的很希望我这样做，底线就是这样。现在，如果我发现你们俩中任何一个有什么太可怕的事情，那可能仍然会是拒绝。但我找不到任何糟糕的事情。

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:52.610
<v Cliff Means>Well, I tell you, one thing that leaned in your favor. I did read something written by you, because I was trying to do as much homework as I could, because my answer was really no. And your writing had made enough white people angry that I said, OK, maybe he's actually trying to do something.
<v Cliff Means>嗯，我告诉你，有一件事倾向于支持你们。我确实读过你写的东西，因为我在尽力做功课，因为我的答案最初真的是拒绝。你的文章让足够多的白人生气了，我说，好吧，也许他真的在试图做点什么。

00:32:52.610 --> 00:33:01.230
<v W. Kamau Bell>So it's clear that Cliff is not exactly a "why can't we just get along" type of guy. And he has very clear thoughts about what should be taught to kids.
<v W. Kamau Bell>所以很明显，Cliff并不完全是那种“我们为什么不能和睦相处”类型的人。而且他对应该教给孩子的东西有非常明确的想法。

00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:04.650
<v W. Kamau Bell>How old was Kadijah when you first started explaining race to her?
<v W. Kamau Bell>你第一次开始向Kadijah解释种族时她几岁？

00:33:04.650 --> 00:33:21.100
<v Cliff Means>I would imagine four or five years old. She was an early talker. We spent a ton of time together, because I asked her, does she want to enter a speech for the Martin Luther King celebration at her school. And we co-wrote a speech that people were very enamored with.
<v Cliff Means>我想四五岁吧。她说话很早。我们花了很多时间在一起，因为我问她，她是否想为学校的马丁·路德·金庆祝活动准备一篇演讲。我们合写了一篇演讲，人们非常着迷。

00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:45.250
<v Cliff Means>And I explained to her that Martin Luther King wasn't killed for the "I Have a Dream" speech, but once he started talking about getting poor whites and poor blacks together, then he had to go. But my children, I taught, you will be going to school, and you will be getting told that there's justice for all. And then when you find out there's not, then you'll be confused. And I don't want my children confused. I want them knowledgeable.
<v Cliff Means>我向她解释说，马丁·路德·金不是因为“我有一个梦想”的演讲而被杀的，而是当他开始谈论把贫穷的白人和贫穷的黑人联合起来时，他就必须消失。但我教我的孩子，你们会上学，你们会被告知人人平等。然后当你发现并不是那样时，你就会感到困惑。我不希望我的孩子困惑。我希望她们知识渊博。

00:33:45.250 --> 00:33:55.030
<v W. Kamau Bell>So at what age did you get into the history of black people in this country and have to cover things like pre-Civil Rights Movement, like Jim Crow and slavery and things like that?
<v W. Kamau Bell>那么你在几岁时开始谈论这个国家的黑人历史，并且不得不涵盖像民权运动之前，像种族隔离和奴隶制之类的事情？

00:33:55.030 --> 00:34:09.110
<v Cliff Means>At five, I taught her about slavery, and I taught her as what it is, that Europe went to war with West Africa for centuries. Kids used to love Native Americans when I was a kid because they fought back. Why didn't black people fight back?
<v Cliff Means>五岁时，我教她关于奴隶制的事，我如实告诉她，欧洲与西非交战了几个世纪。在我小时候，孩子们过去喜欢美洲原住民，因为他们反抗。为什么黑人不反抗？

00:34:09.110 --> 00:34:30.700
<v Cliff Means>Well, they did, and were slaughtered and terrorized and tortured and murdered and raped-- I probably left out rape at that age for her-- captured, kidnapped, and brought over Africans to America. And they were here terrorized and murdered. And we go from that stage all the way to being here, enslaved.
<v Cliff Means>好吧，他们反抗了，然后被屠杀、恐吓、折磨、谋杀和强奸——那个年纪我可能对她略过了强奸——被俘虏、绑架，并被带到美国。他们在这里被恐吓和谋杀。我们从那个阶段一直谈到成为这里被奴役的人。

00:34:30.700 --> 00:34:43.340
<v W. Kamau Bell>He taught her that at five. Cliff is clearly not afraid to throw his kids into the deep end of the racism pool and let them learn to swim. This is pretty much the opposite of what I had done so far. How deep did their racism pool get?
<v W. Kamau Bell>他五岁时就教了她这些。Cliff显然不怕把他的孩子扔进种族主义的深水区，让她们学会游泳。这和我目前所做的几乎相反。她们的种族主义之池有多深？

00:34:43.340 --> 00:34:57.520
<v Cliff Means>She learned things like white officers following the black migration to northern California to work in the shipping yards, in southern California was shipping yards. Hey, we'd better bring some racist white cops here to keep these people in line.
<v Cliff Means>她学到了诸如白人警察跟踪黑人移民到北加州在船厂工作，南加州也有船厂。嘿，我们最好带些种族主义的白人警察来这里让这些人循规蹈矩。

00:34:57.520 --> 00:35:09.450
<v Cliff Means>She learned that there were black cooks grinding up glass in the kitchen to make sure that white racist, brutal slave masters' intestines would bleed and be open, and then he would be gone. What was your question?
<v Cliff Means>她学到了有黑人厨师在厨房里磨碎玻璃，确保白人种族主义、残酷的奴隶主肠子会流血和裂开，然后他就消失了。你的问题是什么？

00:35:09.450 --> 00:35:16.740
<v Cliff Means>[LAUGHTER]
<v Cliff Means>[笑声]

00:35:16.740 --> 00:35:19.660
<v Cliff Means>Do you want the church thing brought up at all?
<v Cliff Means>你想提到教会那件事吗？

00:35:19.660 --> 00:35:21.080
<v Kadijah Means>It's fine.
<v Kadijah Means>没关系。

00:35:21.080 --> 00:35:28.810
<v W. Kamau Bell>That's Kadijah. I don't know what the church thing is, but I can tell it was one of those family stories. Kadijah let my producer, Robyn, and I in on it.
<v W. Kamau Bell>那是Kadijah。我不知道教会那件事是什么，但我能看出这是那些家庭故事之一。Kadijah让我和制作人Robyn知道了。

00:35:28.810 --> 00:35:46.460
<v Kadijah Means>After my dad told me about racism, I distinctly remember being angry with white people, like how dare you enslave my people. And so we were at church one day.
<v Kadijah Means>在我爸爸告诉我关于种族主义的事后，我清楚地记得我对白人感到愤怒，就像你们怎么敢奴役我的人民。所以有一天我们在教堂。

00:35:46.460 --> 00:35:54.060
<v W. Kamau Bell>It wasn't actually at church. It was a church function. It was in the church. It was a church function held after.
<v W. Kamau Bell>其实不是在教堂里。是在教堂举办的活动。是在教堂。那是事后举办的教堂活动。

00:35:54.060 --> 00:36:16.830
<v Kadijah Means>So I guess I went up onto the pulpit and asked for the mic. And I told the entire congregation that black people and white people should not be together. And that was my synthesis of racism in America and slavery that I had come up with throughout the week after my dad told me about racism.
<v Kadijah Means>所以我猜我走上讲台要了麦克风。我告诉整个会众黑人和白人不应该在一起。这就是我从爸爸告诉我种族主义后的一周里总结出来的对美国种族主义和奴隶制的理解。

00:36:16.830 --> 00:36:19.100
<v Robyn>You're saying you became a five-year-old segregationist?
<v Robyn>你是说你成了一个五岁的种族隔离主义者？

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:21.810
<v Kadijah Means>I did. I did.
<v Kadijah Means>是的。是的。

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:34.900
<v W. Kamau Bell>In my house, we can't have a five-year-old segregationist. I mean, don't get me wrong, it'd be fun for a bit in a Kids Say the Darndest Things kind of way. I ran a version of what Kadijah said in church by my wife, Melissa, as a hypothetical.
<v W. Kamau Bell>在我们家，我们不能有一个五岁的种族隔离主义者。我的意思是，别误会，以“小孩说最难听的话”这种方式看一会儿可能会很有趣。我把Kadijah在教堂说的那段话当作一个假设告诉了我妻子Melissa。

00:36:34.900 --> 00:36:53.240
<v W. Kamau Bell>What would you do if Sami told you white people and black people can't be together? It would be an immediate investigation. Melissa would ask, "What makes you say that? Why do you think they can't be together? And yes, OK, white people did hurt your people. But also, Mommy and Daddy are together. And that's good, isn't it?"
<v W. Kamau Bell>如果你女儿Sami告诉你白人和黑人不能在一起，你会怎么做？那会是一场立即的调查。Melissa会问，“你为什么这么说？你为什么认为他们不能在一起？而且是的，好吧，白人确实伤害了你的人民。但妈妈和爸爸也在在一起。那很好，不是吗？”

00:36:53.240 --> 00:37:02.520
<v W. Kamau Bell>It would be a conversation with me. It would be a conversation with Sami, every day, all the time. I asked Cliff how he felt about Kadijah's speech at church.
<v W. Kamau Bell>那会是我和她的对话。那会是我和Sami每天、一直在进行的对话。我问Cliff对Kadijah在教堂的演讲有什么感觉。

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:12.230
<v Cliff Means>I was like, well, she learned what she learned. That's probably a healthy reaction for having learned what she learned this week. I think the audience was a little surprised.
<v Cliff Means>我当时就像，好吧，她学到了她学到的。考虑到她这周学到的东西，这可能是一种健康的反应。我想观众有点惊讶。

00:37:12.230 --> 00:37:20.530
<v W. Kamau Bell>Was there a white person in her life that you felt like, uh-oh, if she thinks that about all white people, then she's going to hate this one person who's actually, you know, a nice person?
<v W. Kamau Bell>她生活中是否有白人让你觉得，哦不，如果她那样看待所有白人，那她会恨这个实际上是好人的白人吗？

00:37:20.530 --> 00:37:51.940
<v Cliff Means>Oh, no, absolutely not. I'm not concerned about the white man in America. I think he's doing very well. And I had no concern, or do I now have any concern for anyone's feelings or whatever else being hurt by a five-year-old. Maybe it could start a conversation with them. They can think about some of the things that they enjoy that no one else enjoy in this country. So no.
<v Cliff Means>哦，不，绝对没有。我不关心美国的白人。我认为他们做得很好。而且我没有，现在也没有任何关心任何人因为五岁小孩的话而感到受伤或无论什么。也许这可以开启他们之间的一个对话。他们可以思考一些他们享受而这个国家其他人没有享受的事情。所以不。

00:37:51.940 --> 00:38:03.430
<v W. Kamau Bell>I really like Cliff. Cliff is not an easy person, but then why should he be easy? Cliff is the type of person who keeps the rest of us a little more honest. I want more Cliff in my life.
<v W. Kamau Bell>我真的很喜欢Cliff。Cliff不是一个容易相处的人，但话说回来，他为什么要容易呢？Cliff是那种让我们其余人保持更诚实的人。我的生活中需要更多像Cliff这样的人。

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:18.500
<v W. Kamau Bell>Kadijah told me that even with her candid, unfiltered upbringing, she could tell early on the difference between white oppression and the white people in her life. But to be able to do that, she had to learn about it and get angry. I get that.
<v W. Kamau Bell>Kadijah告诉我，即使在她那坦率、未经修饰的成长环境下，她也能很早就看出白人压迫与她生活中白人之间的区别。但要做到这一点，她必须去了解并感到愤怒。我明白这点。

00:38:18.500 --> 00:38:32.210
<v W. Kamau Bell>When I first began to really understand the violence of racism, I was angry. It makes sense to be angry. The anger helps you push back against the injustice. Anger is not the goal. It's the fuel.
<v W. Kamau Bell>当我第一次真正开始理解种族主义的暴力时，我很愤怒。感到愤怒是合理的。愤怒帮助你抵制不公。愤怒不是目标。它是燃料。

00:38:32.210 --> 00:38:43.710
<v W. Kamau Bell>Even so, sometimes I feel like I have too much anger. There are black people in America dying from racism. Bad treatment in a fancy coffee shop is the softest and easiest part of racism.
<v W. Kamau Bell>尽管如此，有时我觉得我有太多的愤怒。美国有黑人因种族主义而死亡。在高档咖啡馆里的恶劣待遇只是种族主义最温和、最容易的部分。

00:38:43.710 --> 00:38:49.150
<v W. Kamau Bell>There were times during this whole thing I felt like I should just drop it. Cliff and I talked about that, too.
<v W. Kamau Bell>整个过程中有几次我觉得我应该放弃。Cliff和我也谈过这一点。

00:38:49.150 --> 00:38:59.500
<v W. Kamau Bell>Being told to leave a cafe on College Avenue, that's not a big deal on a basic level. But I knew that you can't just keep letting your boundary get pushed.
<v W. Kamau Bell>被告知离开大学大道的咖啡馆，在基本层面上不是什么大不了的事。但我知道你不能一直让你的界限被推开。

00:38:59.500 --> 00:39:11.300
<v Cliff Means>I don't let anything go. I don't let anything go. Don't shake my hand like you're black or something. You shake my hand like a white man. You shake my hand like you shake-- don't relate to me.
<v Cliff Means>我什么都不放过。我什么都不放过。不要像你是黑人一样跟我握手。你要像白人一样跟我握手。你要像——不要跟我建立关联。

00:39:11.300 --> 00:39:19.310
<v W. Kamau Bell>Don't let anything go is the message of the day. That's what I feel, but I never put it in those words. Those are the words I was looking for. Don't let anything go.
<v W. Kamau Bell>“什么都不放过”是今天的讯息。这就是我的感受，但我从没用这些词表达过。那是我一直在寻找的词。什么都不放过。

00:39:19.310 --> 00:39:44.390
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Racism is real and happening right now, every day. That means that every day I have an opportunity to teach my daughters about racism. So this is to you, Sami and Juno. I promise you, from now on, I'm not going to let it go. The stakes are too high.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>种族主义是真实的，每天都在发生。这意味着每一天我都有机会教我的女儿们关于种族主义的事。所以这是对你们说的，Sami和Juno。我向你们保证，从现在开始，我绝不放过。赌注太大了。

00:39:44.390 --> 00:40:03.010
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>W. Kamau Bell is a comedian in Berkeley. You can hear him on his podcast, "Denzel Washington is the Greatest Actor of All Time Period," or find a list of his upcoming shows on his website, wkamaubell.com. That's W-K-A-M-A-U Bell dot com.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>W. Kamau Bell是伯克利的喜剧演员。你可以在他的播客《丹泽尔·华盛顿是史上最伟大的演员》中听到他，或者在他的网站wkamaubell.com上找到他即将举行的演出列表。即W-K-A-M-A-U Bell点com。

00:40:03.010 --> 00:40:21.250
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Coming up, a group of grownups who are not fumbling at all. They have a plan, and it includes costumes, volcanoes, and a talking stick. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>接下来，一群完全没有笨拙的成年人。他们有一个计划，包括戏服、火山和谈话棒。一分钟后，当我们的节目在芝加哥公共广播电台继续时，你就会知道了。

00:40:21.250 --> 00:40:36.930
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's This American Life. I'm Chana Joffe-Walt, sitting in for Ira Glass. Today's show, "The Birds and the Bees." We are listening in as grownups try to convey their most important deeply-held beliefs to small humans who cannot yet zip up their own jackets.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这是《美国生活》。我是Chana Joffe-Walt，代替Ira Glass主持。今天的节目是《鸟儿与蜜蜂》。我们正在聆听成年人试图向还不会自己拉上夹克拉链的小人类传达他们最重要的核心信念。

00:40:36.930 --> 00:40:49.750
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>We started at the beginning, where do babies come from? In this last part of the show, we're going to talk about the end. Act Three, "About That Farm Upstate." Jonathan Goldstein has this last story.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我们从头开始，婴儿来自哪里？在节目的最后一部分，我们将谈论结束。第三幕，《关于北部的那个农场》。Jonathan Goldstein有最后一个故事。

00:40:49.750 --> 00:41:02.480
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And a quick warning, like I said at the beginning of the show, these are stories about grownups talking with children about difficult subject matter. So if you have kids with you, keep that in mind. OK, here's Jonathan.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>顺便提醒一下，就像我在节目开头所说，这些是关于成年人与孩子谈论困难主题的故事。所以如果你身边有孩子，请记住这一点。好的，这是Jonathan的故事。

00:41:02.480 --> 00:41:18.130
<v Jonathan Goldstein>There's a house in Salt Lake City where kids come to have death explained to them, not just that people die but how they die. It's called The Sharing Place. And the first person they meet there is Jill Macfarlane. This is how she explains a heart attack.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>在盐湖城有一所房子，孩子们到那里去听关于死亡的解释，不仅是人会死，还有他们是如何死的。它叫The Sharing Place。他们在那里见到的第一个人是Jill Macfarlane。这就是她解释心脏病发作的方式。

00:41:18.130 --> 00:41:21.590
<v Jill Macfarlane>Your heart stopped working, and it couldn't move your blood through your body.
<v Jill Macfarlane>你的心脏停止工作了，它无法再将血液泵送到全身。

00:41:21.590 --> 00:41:27.870
<v Jonathan Goldstein>She has a quiver full of kid-sized explanations for all the ways that life can end, like kidney failure.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>她有一大堆适合孩子的解释，说明生命终结的各种方式，比如肾衰竭。

00:41:27.870 --> 00:41:34.160
<v Jill Macfarlane>Your kidneys are washing machines for your blood. And when they stop working and your blood's dirty, then it poisons your body, and you die.
<v Jill Macfarlane>你的肾脏是血液的洗衣机。当它们停止工作而你的血液变脏时，它会毒害你的身体，然后你就死了。

00:41:34.160 --> 00:41:35.170
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Overdoses.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>药物过量。

00:41:35.170 --> 00:41:46.620
<v Jill Macfarlane>Overdose is when you have a sickness in your brain called addiction. An addiction makes you take medicine that's not good for you, and you take way too much of it. But not all medicine's bad either.
<v Jill Macfarlane>药物过量是指你的大脑患有一种叫成瘾的疾病。成瘾会让你服用对你身体不好的药物，而你服用了太多。但也不是所有药物都坏。

00:41:46.620 --> 00:41:48.250
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Have you dealt with murder?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你处理过谋杀案吗？

00:41:48.250 --> 00:42:01.270
<v Jill Macfarlane>Mm-hm. It's when somebody chooses to make your body stop working. It's a choice. We also do choice and not choice. Like, murder is a choice. Cancer is not a choice.
<v Jill Macfarlane>嗯，有。那是有人选择让你的身体停止工作。这是一种选择。我们也谈论选择和非选择。比如，谋杀是一种选择。癌症不是选择。

00:42:01.270 --> 00:42:13.410
<v Jill Macfarlane>We also do contagious or not contagious. Like, you're not going to get my depression. And you're not going to get my cancer.
<v Jill Macfarlane>我们也谈论传染或不传染。比如，你不会得我的抑郁症。你也不会得我的癌症。

00:42:13.410 --> 00:42:28.590
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The Sharing Place is a grief support center for kids who have lost a family member. It's one of hundreds of centers like it around the country. Kids sit in support groups led by grownups, but the point is to allow children to talk to other children about their grief.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The Sharing Place是一个为失去家庭成员的孩子提供的悲伤支持中心。它是全国数百个此类中心之一。孩子们坐在由成年人领导的支持小组中，但目的是让孩子们能够与其他孩子谈论他们的悲伤。

00:42:28.590 --> 00:42:41.860
<v Jonathan Goldstein>They're encouraged to speak in concrete language about death because, the thinking goes, that's how to process death's finality. So people don't pass away. You don't lose them. They die.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>他们被鼓励用具体的语言谈论死亡，因为人们认为，这就是处理死亡不可逆性的方式。所以人们不会“离世”。你不会“失去”他们。他们死了。

00:42:41.860 --> 00:42:54.490
<v Jonathan Goldstein>We invest so much effort trying to shield kids from the scary things in life. We place advisories before TV shows that warn against inappropriate language and subject matter. And what could be less appropriate than death?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我们投入了如此大的努力试图保护孩子免受生活中可怕事物的伤害。我们在电视节目之前放置建议，警告不适当的语言和主题。有什么比死亡更不适当的吗？

00:42:54.490 --> 00:43:08.990
<v Jonathan Goldstein>So for all we let on-- bunnies lay eggs. Your mother means well. And life pretty much goes on forever. To do otherwise feels like you're breaking a basic pact that grownups have with one another.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>所以尽管我们放任——兔子会下蛋。你母亲是为你好。生命几乎永远继续。做其他的事感觉就像你打破了成年人之间的一项基本契约。

00:43:08.990 --> 00:43:11.960
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Who did you have die?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>谁死了？

00:43:11.960 --> 00:43:25.010
<v Child 1>My dad, two guinea pigs, a dog, two cats, and my dad. And that's all.
<v Child 1>我的爸爸，两只豚鼠，一只狗，两只猫，还有我的爸爸。就这样。

00:43:25.010 --> 00:43:44.330
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The kids here are more comfortable talking about death than most adults are, like Hailey, who lost a sister and brother to mitochondrial disease. She and her other brother have the disease, too, but not as bad. They've had to deal with something that most other people haven't had too, let alone other kids. So it's hard to talk about with the kids at school.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>这里的孩子比大多数成年人更乐于谈论死亡，比如Hailey，她因线粒体疾病失去了一个姐姐和一个哥哥。她和她另一个哥哥也患有这种病，只是没那么严重。他们不得不面对大多数人甚至其他孩子从未遇到过的事情。所以很难与学校的孩子们谈论。

00:43:44.330 --> 00:44:03.890
<v Hailey>Sometimes they don't even know what we're talking about, because we know all this stuff, like all these words that none of the other kids heard about, like neurological or something like that. And they're like, what does that mean? Or like a feeding tube, what does that mean?
<v Hailey>有时他们甚至不知道我们在谈论什么，因为我们知道所有这些东西，比如所有其他孩子没听说过的词，比如“神经系统”之类的。他们就会说，那是什么意思？或者像喂食管，那是什么意思？

00:44:03.890 --> 00:44:05.990
<v Child 2>It's like really hard to explain.
<v Child 2>这真的很难解释。

00:44:05.990 --> 00:44:15.470
<v Hailey>It's not hard to explain, but it's hard to tell them, and they still don't understand where it goes or what it does to help you.
<v Hailey>不是很难解释，但很难告诉他们，而且他们仍然不明白它去哪里或者它是如何帮助你的。

00:44:15.470 --> 00:44:31.630
<v Jonathan Goldstein>But this is a place where kids do understand. There are more than a dozen separate the groups at The Sharing Place, broken down by age. The groups meet twice a month for about an hour and a half in a refurbished house, made to feel homey and safe so it's like visiting your grandma's.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>但这是一个孩子们确实能理解的地方。The Sharing Place有十几组独立的小组，按年龄划分。小组每两周在房子里开会一次，大约一个半小时，房子经过翻新，让人感到舒适安全，就像去奶奶家一样。

00:44:31.630 --> 00:44:45.780
<v Jonathan Goldstein>There are rooms where kids meet and talk and rooms where their parents do the same. But most of the rooms are dedicated to play. There's a costume room where kids can dress up like police, ballerinas, cowboys.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>有房间供孩子们见面聊天，也有房间供他们的父母做同样的事。但大多数房间致力于游戏。有一个戏服室，孩子们可以打扮成警察、芭蕾舞演员、牛仔。

00:44:45.780 --> 00:44:51.670
<v Child 3>I'm Spider-Man. So you can do whatever I tell you to do.
<v Child 3>我是蜘蛛侠。所以你可以做我告诉你的任何事。

00:44:51.670 --> 00:45:04.820
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And beside it is a room called the soft room, which is full of props and toys, including an old telephone. One little girl who watched her mother have a heart attack spent an entire play session pretending to call for help.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>旁边是一个叫“软软屋”的房间，里面充满了道具和玩具，包括一部旧电话。一个小女孩目睹了她母亲心脏病发作，她花了一整段游戏时间假装拨打求救电话。

00:45:04.820 --> 00:45:21.530
<v Jonathan Goldstein>"Hi, 9-1-1," she'd say. "My grandma's dying. Hurry. Come quick." Then she'd hang up and dial again. "Hi, 9-1-1, my dad's dying. Hurry. Come quick."
<v Jonathan Goldstein>“你好，911，”她说。“我奶奶快不行了。快点。快过来。”然后她会挂断电话再次拨打。“你好，911，我爸爸快不行了。快点。快过来。”

00:45:21.530 --> 00:45:25.350
<v Jonathan Goldstein>One of the most popular rooms is a padded room called the volcano room.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>最受欢迎的房间之一是一个叫“火山房”的软垫房间。

00:45:25.350 --> 00:45:26.810
<v Child 4>I'm going to go play in the volcano room.
<v Child 4>我要去火山房玩。

00:45:26.810 --> 00:45:39.600
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It's full of cushions and large rubber yoga balls. It's even got an adult-sized dummy to wrestle and punch. The kids can scream, yell, pound the walls, and throw things around. They love it.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>里面满是靠垫和大的橡胶瑜伽球。它甚至有一个成人大小的假人供人摔打和猛击。孩子们可以尖叫、大喊、捶墙、到处扔东西。他们很喜欢。

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:46.460
<v Child 4>Whoo. Bling. Boom. Bam. No ball can attack me. Whoo! Boing.
<v Child 4>呜。砰。蹦。砸。没有球能攻击我。呜！蹦。

00:45:46.460 --> 00:45:57.890
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It's a release. Eve is nine years old, and her father died about two years ago. She knows that feeling of keeping it all bottled up inside. She says she can go days at school like that.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>这是一种释放。Eve九岁了，她父亲大约两年前去世。她知道那种把一切都憋在心里的感觉。她说她可以在学校那样过几天。

00:45:57.890 --> 00:46:15.220
<v Eve>It kind of feels really overwhelming. And it's like you don't know what to do. And on the inside, it's like hurting somehow in a feeling way. It like hurts somehow.
<v Eve>感觉真的很有压力。就像你不知道该怎么办。而在内心深处，就像以某种感觉的方式受到伤害。它确实以某种方式受伤。

00:46:15.220 --> 00:46:18.180
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Where would you say it hurts?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你会说它哪里受伤了？

00:46:18.180 --> 00:46:34.840
<v Eve>Usually, with me, it would hurt in the throat and kind of in my stomach. It's like something that needs to come out, but just I really need to learn how to do that.
<v Eve>通常，对我来说，它会在喉咙和胃部受伤。就像有什么东西需要出来，但我真的需要学习如何做到这一点。

00:46:34.840 --> 00:46:44.190
<v Nancy Reiser>I think there are people in this world who can grow up and don't really know how to talk frankly about lots of things.
<v Nancy Reiser>我想世界上有些人可以长大，却真的不知道如何坦率地谈论很多事情。

00:46:44.190 --> 00:46:51.270
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Nancy Reiser is one of the co-founders of The Sharing Place. And this is what she says when children ask her how people die.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Nancy Reiser是The Sharing Place的联合创始人之一。当孩子们问她人们是如何死亡时，她就是这样说的。

00:46:51.270 --> 00:47:09.720
<v Nancy Reiser>I say, well, close your eyes and listen. [HEAVY BREATHING]
<v Nancy Reiser>我说，嗯，闭上眼睛听着。[沉重的呼吸声]

00:47:09.720 --> 00:47:18.170
<v Nancy Reiser>And there isn't the next breath. Does it hurt? I don't think so. I don't think so. Their face doesn't look like it hurt.
<v Nancy Reiser>然后就没有下一次呼吸了。它痛吗？我不这么认为。我不这么认为。他们的脸看起来不像很痛。

00:47:18.170 --> 00:47:35.690
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Nancy's a therapist who works with young children. For a long time, the very concept that children do, in fact, grieve was hotly disputed. Freud said that it was difficult for children to even conceive of such loss. And as a culture, Nancy says, that's pretty much the idea that we operate under.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Nancy是一位与幼儿合作的治疗师。很长一段时间里，孩子们确实会悲伤这一概念一直存在激烈争议。弗洛伊德说，孩子甚至很难构想出这样的损失。作为一个文化，Nancy说，这几乎就是我们运作的基础理念。

00:47:35.690 --> 00:47:47.430
<v Nancy Reiser>Oh, look, she's playing. She's OK. She didn't really understand what happened. Well, she's playing. But then half an hour later, she's in the house, holding her teddy bear and sucking her thumb and crying.
<v Nancy Reiser>哦，看，她在玩耍。她很好。她并没有真正理解发生了什么。但半小时后，她在房子里，抱着她的泰迪熊，吮吸着手指，哭泣。

00:47:47.430 --> 00:48:08.700
<v Nancy Reiser>So we need to let people know that children grieve, and help them when they grieve so it's not stuck inside and comes out when they try to have a relationship later. When they start to get intimate, they cannot bear it, and they go off. And they don't know why.
<v Nancy Reiser>所以我们需要让人们知道孩子们会悲伤，并在他们悲伤时帮助他们，这样悲伤就不会困在心里，导致他们在以后建立关系时爆发。当他们开始亲密时，他们无法承受，然后他们就离开了。而他们不知道为什么。

00:48:08.700 --> 00:48:19.230
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Nancy says children just grieve differently than adults, especially little children. They grieve in fits and starts. They can't focus on it for very long. And grief is more physical for them.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Nancy说孩子们的悲伤方式与成年人不同，尤其是幼儿。他们的悲伤是断断续续的。他们无法长时间专注。而且对他们来说，悲伤更具身体性。

00:48:19.230 --> 00:48:33.160
<v Jonathan Goldstein>They'll act out their anger, maybe kick a door, which is the reason for the volcano room at The Sharing Place. They might also regress, suddenly using baby talk or sucking their thumbs. And if they're potty trained, they might become untrained.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>他们会发泄愤怒，也许踢门，这就是The Sharing Place有火山房的原因。他们也可能退化，突然使用婴儿语或吮吸手指。如果他们已经戒了尿布，他们可能会重新尿床。

00:48:33.160 --> 00:49:00.230
<v Jonathan Goldstein>They're also magical thinkers. I heard stories of kids who were afraid to go to sleep because grandma went to sleep and didn't wake up. One little boy wandered away from his mom at the emergency room saying, I'm looking for Dad. We left him here last time. Another boy said he just wanted to die for a few days so he can go to heaven and teach his little sister how to ride a tricycle.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>他们也是魔法思维者。我听说过孩子们害怕睡觉的故事，因为奶奶睡着了就没醒来。一个小男孩在急诊室从他妈妈身边走开说，我在找爸爸。上次我们把他留在这里了。另一个男孩说他只想死几天，这样他就能去天堂教他妹妹骑三轮车了。

00:49:00.230 --> 00:49:15.840
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Children also re-grieve. That is, with every new stage of development, they experience their grief anew. And with every milestone-- when their braces come off, when they get their driver's license, when they graduate-- they'll inevitably think, I wish my mom was here.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>孩子们也会重新悲伤。也就是说，随着每个新的发展阶段，他们会重新体验他们的悲伤。随着每一个里程碑——当他们的牙套拆掉时，当他们拿到驾照时，当他们毕业时——他们不可避免地会想，我希望我妈妈在这里。

00:49:15.840 --> 00:49:30.940
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And given all of this, the thought behind The Sharing Place and other centers like it is that kids can help one another in a way that adults perhaps can't help them. That's why they're brought together in these groups. In short, kids speak the same language.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>鉴于所有这些，The Sharing Place及其他中心背后的想法是，孩子们可以以一种成年人可能无法帮助他们的方式互相帮助。这就是为什么他们被聚在一起的原因。简而言之，孩子说同样的语言。

00:49:30.940 --> 00:49:32.410
<v Gavin>Is that thing turned on?
<v Gavin>那个东西开着吗？

00:49:32.410 --> 00:49:34.700
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It is turned on. What do you think about that?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>开着。你怎么看？

00:49:34.700 --> 00:49:48.290
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Before their session at The Sharing Place, I sit down with Gavin, who's six, and his brother, Aidan, who's eight. They're both dressed in matching long-sleeved polo shirts. Gavin sits on his mother Nichole's lap. Aidan sits beside them.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在他们参加The Sharing Place的会议之前，我和六岁的Gavin以及他八岁的哥哥Aidan坐在一起。他们都穿着配套的长袖Polo衫。Gavin坐在他妈妈Nichole的腿上。Aidan坐在他们旁边。

00:49:48.290 --> 00:49:50.350
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Oh, do you want to introduce yourself?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>哦，你想自我介绍一下吗？

00:49:50.350 --> 00:49:57.130
<v Aidan>Hi. I'm Aidan. My dad died by--
<v Aidan>嗨。我是Aidan。我爸爸死于——

00:49:57.130 --> 00:49:57.530
<v Nichole>Suicide.
<v Nichole>自杀。

00:49:57.530 --> 00:49:59.900
<v Aidan>Suicide.
<v Aidan>自杀。

00:49:59.900 --> 00:50:06.090
<v Jonathan Goldstein>His little brother, Gavin, points his finger to his head and pulls the pretend trigger. He snaps is head back.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>他弟弟Gavin把手指指向自己的头，扣动了假想的扳机。他头向后一仰。

00:50:06.090 --> 00:50:07.570
