<v Nichole>是的，那就是发生的。

00:50:07.570 --> 00:50:09.370
<v Jonathan Goldstein>What did you just do there?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你刚才做了什么？

00:50:09.370 --> 00:50:16.360
<v Gavin>He shot his head. And then, we didn't know he was dead, right Mama?
<v Gavin>他射了自己的头。然后，我们不知道他死了，对吧妈妈？

00:50:16.360 --> 00:50:17.900
<v Nichole>We didn't.
<v Nichole>我们不知道。

00:50:17.900 --> 00:50:21.980
<v Gavin>And then we said, "Wake up, Dad."
<v Gavin>然后我们说，“醒醒，爸爸。”

00:50:21.980 --> 00:50:28.100
<v Nichole>No, you didn't see him. Mommy found him. And he was already dead.
<v Nichole>不，你们没看见他。妈妈发现了他。他已经死了。

00:50:28.100 --> 00:50:30.110
<v Gavin>Did you turn him over?
<v Gavin>你把他翻过来了吗？

00:50:30.110 --> 00:50:32.220
<v Nichole>I didn't touch him.
<v Nichole>我没碰他。

00:50:32.220 --> 00:50:32.400
<v Gavin>OK.
<v Gavin>好的。

00:50:32.400 --> 00:50:40.820
<v Aidan>Because she knew he was dead, because she saw blood and things.
<v Aidan>因为她知道他死了，因为她看到了血之类的东西。

00:50:40.820 --> 00:50:41.450
<v Jonathan Goldstein>On his head?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>在他头上？

00:50:41.450 --> 00:50:55.910
<v Aidan>Yeah. And he was a terrific guy-- so nice, so generous. He would always help anyone. He was a terrific guy.
<v Aidan>是的。他是个了不起的人——非常善良，非常慷慨。他总是乐于助人。他是个了不起的人。

00:50:55.910 --> 00:50:59.490
<v Nichole>What did you learn here that helped you the most?
<v Nichole>你在这里学到的哪一点对你帮助最大？

00:50:59.490 --> 00:51:02.770
<v Aidan>To understand how he died.
<v Aidan>理解他是怎么死的。

00:51:02.770 --> 00:51:19.030
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Sometimes that task, telling the kids that their parent committed suicide, falls to Jill Macfarlane, because the surviving parent or guardian might be so inside their own grief that they can't bring themselves to do it. Jill explains suicide as simply as she does other kinds of death.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>有时这项任务，告诉孩子他们的父母自杀了，落在了Jill Macfarlane身上，因为幸存的父母或监护人可能深陷自己的悲伤中，无法开口。Jill解释自杀的方式和解释其他死亡方式一样简单。

00:51:19.030 --> 00:51:32.090
<v Jonathan Goldstein>There's a sickness in your brain called depression, she says. And it can make you decide to make your own body stop working. She says it's required that the kids know how their brother or sister or mother died before starting the group.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你的大脑患有一种叫抑郁症的疾病，她说。它能让你决定让自己的身体停止工作。她说，在开始小组活动之前，要求孩子们知道他们的兄弟姐妹或母亲是如何死亡的。

00:51:32.090 --> 00:51:42.930
<v Jill Macfarlane>Well, I had one family say he died because he was sick. One family said that he died in a car accident. One family said that he accidentally shot himself. He didn't mean to.
<v Jill Macfarlane>嗯，有一个家庭说他因为生病去世了。一个家庭说他在车祸中丧生。一个家庭说他不小心射中自己。他不是故意的。

00:51:42.930 --> 00:51:51.040
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It was Jill who helped their mom break the news to Aidan and Gavin that their dad shot himself. She says that conversation was especially hard.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>是Jill帮助他们的母亲把他们父亲开枪自杀的消息告诉了Aidan和Gavin。她说那次谈话特别困难。

00:51:51.040 --> 00:51:57.510
<v Jill Macfarlane>The boys immediately said, you don't know my dad. Why would you say that? You don't know us.
<v Jill Macfarlane>男孩们立即说，你不认识我爸爸。你为什么这么说？你不认识我们。

00:51:57.510 --> 00:52:08.750
<v Jill Macfarlane>And I said, I know. I don't know you. But your mom's here today because she needed my help in telling you this, because this is a really hard and scary thing that happened. And your mom told me that that's what happened.
<v Jill Macfarlane>我说，我知道。我不认识你们。但你妈妈今天在这儿，因为她需要我的帮助来告诉你们这件事，因为这是一个非常困难和可怕的事情。你妈妈告诉我那是发生的。

00:52:08.750 --> 00:52:18.970
<v Jill Macfarlane>And Aidan just was like, you're crazy, lady. There's no way that we're going to talk about this. This is not what happened. It was horrible and was awful. And I cried that whole night.
<v Jill Macfarlane>Aidan就像，你疯了，女士。我们不可能谈论这个。这不是发生的。那是可怕的，那是糟糕的。我那天晚上哭了一整夜。

00:52:18.970 --> 00:52:27.720
<v Jill Macfarlane>It was just awful. I just felt so horrible. And my husband was like, you can't do this. You have to go to the director and tell her that you can't do this again.
<v Jill Macfarlane>那真的太糟糕了。我感到太可怕了。我丈夫就像，你不能这样做。你必须去告诉主任你不能再这样做了。

00:52:27.720 --> 00:52:38.950
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Suicide is always one of the highest, if not the highest cause of death for Sharing Place families at any given time. There's actually a special group devoted specifically to suicide.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>无论何时，自杀始终是The Sharing Place家庭死亡的主要原因，如果不是主要原因的话。实际上有一个专门针对自杀的特殊小组。

00:52:38.950 --> 00:52:50.990
<v Jonathan Goldstein>And a lot of the stories you hear in that group are so horrible that you can't help but wonder, why tell the kids what happened at all? Why not just say they died and leave it at that?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你在那个小组里听到的很多故事都太可怕了，你忍不住想，为什么要告诉孩子发生了什么？为什么不只是说他们死了然后就此打住呢？

00:52:50.990 --> 00:53:07.680
<v Jonathan Goldstein>According to Jill and her colleagues, we need to tell children so they won't find out later in life and wonder, what else did they lie to me about? They also don't want to have to cloak it in a way that suggests it's unspeakable and shameful. They get enough of that idea at school.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>根据Jill和她同事的说法，我们需要告诉孩子们，这样他们长大后就不会发现真相并想，他们还对我撒了什么谎？他们也不想以一种暗示这是不可言说且令人羞耻的方式来掩盖它。他们在学校里已经受够了那种想法。

00:53:07.680 --> 00:53:11.140
<v Jonathan Goldstein>This is Eve again, whose father also shot himself.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>这是Eve，她父亲也是开枪自杀的。

00:53:11.140 --> 00:53:32.220
<v Eve>Well, some people at school, they say this makes me feel really sad, too. They say that when somebody kills themselves, that means they go to the wrong place. I don't believe that, but it just makes me feel really sad when they say that. Like instead of going to a happy place, they would go to a sad.
<v Eve>嗯，学校里有些人，他们说这让我感觉很难过。他们说当有人自杀时，那意味着他们去了错误的地方。我不相信，但当他们那样说时，确实让我感觉很难过。就像他们没去一个快乐的地方，而是去了一个悲伤的地方。

00:53:32.220 --> 00:53:38.560
<v Jonathan Goldstein>But even if you don't believe it, and you know it's nonsense, it still kind of hurts that someone would say that?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>但即使你不相信，而且你知道那是无稽之谈，有人那样说还是会让人受伤？

00:53:38.560 --> 00:54:06.590
<v Eve>Yeah, it does, because it's like somebody judging my papa, which it's not a bad thing. A lot of people do that. But you know, just kind of feels a little weird. Like on the inside, it makes me feel like I'm going to scream and do something like that.
<v Eve>是的，确实会，因为就像有人在评判我爸爸，那不是一件坏事。很多人都那样做。但你知道，感觉有点怪怪的。在内心深处，让我感觉我想尖叫，想做那样的事情。

00:54:06.590 --> 00:54:11.610
<v Jonathan Goldstein>What are the things that you wish that people would say?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你希望人们说什么？

00:54:11.610 --> 00:54:34.960
<v Eve>Probably it's OK that that has happened. And we can help you with feeling better and understanding you. That's probably something I'd want somebody to say.
<v Eve>可能就是，发生了那样的事没关系。我们可以帮助你感觉更好并理解你。那可能是我希望有人说的。

00:54:34.960 --> 00:54:44.600
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Once a child knows how their loved one died, they're encouraged to say it out loud. There's a kind of sorcery to it, naming the dragon so you can defeat it.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>一旦孩子知道他们心爱的人是如何死亡的，他们就被鼓励大声说出来。这有一种魔法，命名那条龙，这样你就能击败它。

00:54:44.600 --> 00:55:04.580
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I sat in on the suicide group, which is different than the other groups in that kids tend to come to meetings for a longer period of time, partly because they didn't get to say goodbye. The kids sit a circle at the beginning of the meeting and hand around a talking stick. One by one, they say their name, who died, and how they died.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我参加了自杀小组，这与其他小组不同，孩子们往往会参加更长时间的会议，部分原因是他们没能说再见。孩子们在会议开始时围成一个圈，传递一个谈话棒。一个接一个，他们说出自己的名字，谁死了，以及他们是怎么死的。

00:55:04.580 --> 00:55:22.140
<v Elias>My name is Elias, and the person who died was my dad. And he died by shooting his self with a gun. And he wanted to die.
<v Elias>我叫Elias，死的人是我爸爸。他用枪射击自己死了。而且他想死。

00:55:22.140 --> 00:55:35.230
<v Lindsey>Hello, my name is Lindsey. My dad died. He died by suicide. And I regret not seeing him, because I hadn't seen him for a couple of months before his death.
<v Lindsey>你好，我叫Lindsey。我爸爸死了。他是自杀的。我后悔没见到他，因为在他去世前几个月我没见过他。

00:55:35.230 --> 00:55:45.080
<v Jonathan Goldstein>There's also a different question they respond to each time they meet. What do you miss most about the person? Is there anything you don't miss? Tonight's question is, what do you regret?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>他们每次聚会时回答的问题也不一样。你最怀念那个人的什么？有什么你不怀念的吗？今晚的问题是，你后悔什么？

00:55:45.080 --> 00:56:00.940
<v Ethan>My name is Ethan, and the person who died is my dad. He died by shooting his self in the head. And I don't know if I have any regrets. I just can't remember.
<v Ethan>我叫Ethan，死的人是我爸爸。他朝自己脑袋开枪死了。我不知道我有没有什么遗憾。我只是记不起来了。

00:56:00.940 --> 00:56:14.330
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It can take a long time for them to get to this point, where they can say the word suicide. The Sharing Place never corrects any of the kids or forces them to say anything they don't want to. But they notice when a child is finally able to say it.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>他们可能需要很长时间才能达到这一点，能够说出“自杀”这个词。The Sharing Place从不纠正任何孩子或强迫他们说任何他们不想说的话。但他们会注意到一个孩子终于能说出来的时候。

00:56:14.330 --> 00:56:21.610
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The night I was there, Aidan, the nine-year-old I talked to who had a hard time saying the word suicide, was finally able to say it in group.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我在的那晚，Aidan，那个我交谈过的九岁孩子，他很难说出“自杀”这个词，终于能在小组里说出来了。

00:56:21.610 --> 00:56:22.820
<v Volunteer 1>So how about Aidan?
<v Volunteer 1>那么Aidan怎么样？

00:56:22.820 --> 00:56:25.840
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The volunteers sat around and talked about it afterwards.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>志愿者们围坐在一起事后讨论。

00:56:25.840 --> 00:56:35.160
<v Volunteer 1>It was hard for him, I could tell. He wasn't about to say it, but he said it. And I was proud of him. But it took him probably at least a minute to actually get those words out.
<v Volunteer 1>对他来说很难，我看得出来。他本来不想说，但他说了。我为他感到骄傲。但他可能至少花了一分钟才真正把那些词说出来。

00:56:35.160 --> 00:56:37.800
<v Volunteer 2>And he had to ask his mom if it was OK to say.
<v Volunteer 2>而且他必须问他妈妈说出来是否可以。

00:56:37.800 --> 00:56:39.250
<v Volunteer 3>So she wasn't coaching him?
<v Volunteer 3>所以她没有在指导他？

00:56:39.250 --> 00:56:39.670
<v Volunteer 1>Nope.
<v Volunteer 1>没有。

00:56:39.670 --> 00:56:42.380
<v Volunteer 2>No. He was good.
<v Volunteer 2>不。他表现得很好。

00:56:42.380 --> 00:56:54.060
<v Jonathan Goldstein>On the other hand, Aidan's brother, Gavin, the six-year-old, talks about his dad's suicide incessantly. He also draws guns during playtime and even sculpts them out of Play-Doh. This is Jill Macfarlane again.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>另一方面，Aidan的弟弟Gavin，那个六岁的孩子，不停地谈论他爸爸的自杀。他在玩耍时也画枪，甚至用橡皮泥雕塑枪。这是Jill Macfarlane。

00:56:54.060 --> 00:57:04.850
<v Jill Macfarlane>He tells everybody at school, "My dad shot his brains." And it freaks everybody out. And so then he gets in trouble at school, which then he was like, well, I guess I can't ever talk about my dad.
<v Jill Macfarlane>他告诉学校里的每个人，“我爸爸射了自己的大脑。”这让每个人都吓坏了。所以他在学校惹了麻烦，然后他就像，好吧，我想我永远不能谈论我爸爸了。

00:57:04.850 --> 00:57:11.110
<v Jill Macfarlane>But this is how kindergartners talk and process things. It's just easy for them to talk about. But it scares other people.
<v Jill Macfarlane>但这正是幼儿园孩子说话和处理事情的方式。对他们来说谈论这些很容易。但这会吓到其他人。

00:57:11.110 --> 00:57:14.470
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Is it OK for other kids in their class to hear that kind of thing?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>班上的其他孩子听到那种话没关系吗？

00:57:14.470 --> 00:57:19.480
<v Jill Macfarlane>Well, yeah. I mean, is there a right answer to that? I don't know.
<v Jill Macfarlane>嗯，是的。我的意思是，有正确的答案吗？我不知道。

00:57:19.480 --> 00:57:43.130
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I don't know either. If I were a parent, I'm not sure I'd want my kid hearing that kind of thing at school. And while Jill doesn't have an answer, she does think kids being allowed to express themselves is much healthier than sending them a message that it's wrong to say this kind of stuff out loud. And that's got to be a step in the right direction.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我也不知道。如果我是父母，我不确定我是否想让我的孩子在学校听到那种话。虽然Jill没有答案，但她确实认为，允许孩子表达自己远比向他们传达“说出这类话是错误的”这种信息要健康得多。那一定是朝着正确方向迈出的一步。

00:57:43.130 --> 00:57:56.270
<v Sara Mohammed>I have very, very, very fond memories of this place. I actually tried to come through the side door, because that was the door that I always came in through. Then I was like, I can use the adult entrance, finally.
<v Sara Mohammed>我对这个地方有非常、非常、非常美好的回忆。我实际上试着从侧门进来，因为那是我总是进来的门。然后我心想，我终于可以使用成人入口了。

00:57:56.270 --> 00:58:05.330
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Sara Mohammed hasn't been to The Sharing Place for a full six years. She's 21 now. But when she was 11, her older brother hanged himself.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Sara Mohammed已经六年没来过The Sharing Place了。她现在21岁了。但当她11岁时，她哥哥上吊自杀了。

00:58:05.330 --> 00:58:20.210
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Even though he was supposed to be babysitting her, her parents had told her to keep an eye out on him because of his past suicide attempts. Obviously what happened wasn't her fault, but she blamed herself, which is something I heard other kids here talk about too.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>尽管他本应照顾她，但她父母告诉她要留意他，因为他有过自杀倾向。显然发生的事不是她的错，但她责怪自己，这是我听到其他孩子也谈论的事情。

00:58:20.210 --> 00:58:30.450
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It's easy for an adult to say, don't be silly. It's not your fault. But it's a whole other thing to talk with a kid at The Sharing Place who says, I know exactly what you're talking about.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>成年人说“别傻了，这不是你的错”很容易。但与在The Sharing Place中说“我完全明白你在说什么”的孩子交谈是另一回事。

00:58:30.450 --> 00:58:56.480
<v Sara Mohammed>It was kind of nice seeing the older kids whose loved ones had been gone for a few years, how they were able to go to school normally and just live their normal lives, because I just didn't want to go to school. I didn't want to be with kids who just didn't know. It was too hard.
<v Sara Mohammed>看到那些心爱的人已经离开几年的大孩子能够像正常人一样上学并过正常生活，感觉很好，因为我当时根本不想上学。我不想和那些什么都不知道的孩子在一起。太难了。

00:58:56.480 --> 00:59:06.230
<v Jonathan Goldstein>I asked Sara if there was a turning point, a moment when she began to feel like maybe she was doing a little better. She said, yes. She was in the courtyard with a bunch of other kids.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>我问Sara是否有转折点，那一刻她开始觉得也许她好转了一点。她说，是的。她和一群其他孩子在庭院里。

00:59:06.230 --> 00:59:51.320
<v Sara Mohammed>We were just out there painting rocks or something for a fundraiser. And we were just talking, just talking like normal. And I realized that I hadn't had a conversation like that in about a year, just where I felt 100% free, and just laughing and not feeling guilty about laughing, and just happy. And yeah, that was really the first time in a good nine months after my brother had passed that I felt OK.
<v Sara Mohammed>我们只是在那里画石头之类为筹款做准备。我们只是在谈话，就像正常人一样谈话。我意识到我大约一年没进行过那样的谈话了，就是我觉得百分之百自由，只是大笑而不会因为大笑感到内疚，只是快乐。是的，那确实是我哥哥去世后九个月里第一次感觉还好。

00:59:51.320 --> 01:00:04.240
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It's the kids themselves who decide when they're ready to stop coming to The Sharing Place. But on average, they stay about two years. When they're ready to leave, they have to announce their intention two times in group before their final goodbye.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>是孩子们自己决定什么时候准备停止来The Sharing Place。但平均来说，他们待大约两年。当他们准备离开时，他们在小组里必须宣布两次他们的意图，然后才能进行最后的告别。

01:00:04.240 --> 01:00:08.190
<v Volunteer 4>Any of you that would like to say something to him, which I'm sure several of you will?
<v Volunteer 4>你们中有谁想对他说话吗，我肯定你们中很多人都会？

01:00:08.190 --> 01:00:26.440
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The goodbye is ceremonial. And on the last night I was there, at the end of the meeting, they said goodbye to a kid name Robby. He sat in the circle with the other kids and volunteers. And anybody who wanted to could pick up the talking stick and say a few words. His friend, Jessica, was the last kid to do so.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>告别是仪式性的。我在那里的最后一个晚上，会议结束时，他们向一个叫Robby的孩子道别。他和其他孩子及志愿者坐在圈子里。任何想说话的人都可以拿起谈话棒说几句。他的朋友Jessica是最后一个这么做的孩子。

01:00:26.440 --> 01:00:39.000
<v Jessica>I'll definitely miss you, because you're like one of my best friends in this group. And I'll miss seeing you. And our major fight that we had in the volcano room, it was not my fault.
<v Jessica>我一定会想念你的，因为你就像我在这个小组里最好的朋友之一。我会想念看到你的。我们在火山房打的那场大架，那不是我的错。

01:00:39.000 --> 01:00:39.690
<v Robby>You asked for it.
<v Robby>是你自找的。

01:00:39.690 --> 01:00:40.750
<v Jessica>No I didn't.
<v Jessica>不，我没。

01:00:40.750 --> 01:00:41.960
<v Robby>Yes, you did.
<v Robby>是的，你有。

01:00:41.960 --> 01:00:49.570
<v Jessica>No. It was just really fun, and I'm going to miss seeing you and miss hanging out with you.
<v Jessica>不。那真的很有趣，我会想念你的，想念和你一起玩。

01:00:49.570 --> 01:01:01.510
<v Jonathan Goldstein>At Robby's last meeting, there was a kid who'd just shown up. It was his first meeting. He had long bangs hanging over his eyes and, except for his name, didn't say anything the whole time.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>在Robby最后一次会议上，有一个刚来的孩子。这是他第一次来。他长长的刘海垂在眼睛上，除了名字，他整个过程中一句话也没说。

01:01:01.510 --> 01:01:37.570
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Whenever a new person joins, the veteran kids have a chance to gauge their own progress, to remember what they were like when they first showed up, and think about how bad it used to be, and how now it isn't as bad. And ultimately, like Robby, they'll make way for someone on the long waiting list, a list on which new names are added all the time. They'll say, "I think it's time to go." And then they'll say it again.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>每当有新人加入，老学员们就有机会衡量自己的进步，记住他们刚到时是什么样子，思考过去有多糟，而现在没那么糟了。最终，就像Robby一样，他们会给漫长的等待名单上的人腾出空间，那是一份名单，新的名字一直都在增加。他们会说，“我想是时候走了。”然后他们会再说一次。

01:01:37.570 --> 01:01:42.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Jonathan Goldstein is host of the CBC radio show and podcast, Wiretap.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Jonathan Goldstein是CBC电台节目和播客《Wiretap》的主持人。

